The linker errors indicate that several `regulator_fake_..._reset` and `regulator_fake_..._fake` symbols are undefined, as well as `__device_dts_ord_XX` symbols.

1.  **`regulator_fake_...` undefined references**: These symbols are part of the `fff` (Fake Function Framework) library used for testing. They are defined when the `zephyr,regulator-fake` driver is compiled and linked. The `regulator_fake.c` source file (part of the Zephyr RTOS) contains the actual definitions for these fake functions and their associated data structures. If no device in the devicetree uses the `zephyr,regulator-fake` compatible, then this driver's source file will not be included in the build, leading to these linker errors.

2.  **`__device_dts_ord_XX` undefined references**: These symbols represent the device objects generated by Zephyr's device driver model based on the devicetree. If a devicetree node lacks a `compatible` property, Zephyr cannot associate it with a driver, and thus no device object will be generated or linked for that node. The errors specifically mention `__device_dts_ord_40` (corresponding to `reg0` or `regulator`) and `__device_dts_ord_43` (corresponding to `reg3`).

The solution is to explicitly assign the `zephyr,regulator-fake` compatible to all the regulator nodes in the `app.overlay` that are being accessed as devices in `src/main.c`. This will ensure that the `zephyr,regulator-fake` driver is compiled and linked, providing the necessary fake function definitions and device objects.

