The linker errors indicate that the fake functions (e.g., `regulator_fake_get_error_flags_reset`, `regulator_fake_get_error_flags_fake`) used by the `fff` mocking framework are not defined. This typically happens when the `FAKE_VALUE_FUNC` or `FAKE_VOID_FUNC` macros are not invoked for the functions being faked.

Although `zephyr/drivers/regulator/fake.h` is included, it appears it does not properly declare these fakes in a way that generates their definitions in `main.c`. To resolve this, we must explicitly declare all the faked functions using `FAKE_VALUE_FUNC` macros directly in `main.c`, immediately after `DEFINE_FFF_GLOBALS;`. This ensures that the `fff` framework generates the necessary fake function definitions and their associated data structures, resolving the "undefined reference" errors.

The `__device_dts_ord_XX` errors are likely a secondary symptom, as the linker cannot resolve references to device pointers when the `fff` fake structures (which hold these arguments) are themselves undefined. Fixing the `fff` declarations should resolve these as well.

